  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 201 PM MDT Fri Jul 31 2026\n\nSynoptically, not much has changed for the Long Term period. \nSunday the ridge will continue to de-amplify as a trough begins \nto move through Canada/US border. Come Monday, we begin to \ntransition to more zonal flow. Once again throughout the week, \nthere looks to be some shortwaves moving through the area that \nmay create some precipitation chances for the County Warning \nArea (CWA).\n\nOverall for the long term, high temperatures are forecast to be\nin the high 90s for the majority of the CWA. However Wednesday \nwe have a brief \"cool\" down where highs should be in the low to \nmid 90s. Winds for the week look to be breezy for the majority \nof the CWA. Starting on Monday, the CWA's precipitation chances \nbegin to increase. As each shortwave moves through, there is \naround a 20-40% chance for showers Monday through Wednesday. \nThursday and Friday chances begin to decrease to 10-20%.\n\nFor this period there are some localized elevated fire weather \nconcerns for Sunday and Monday during the afternoon hours. \nBeginning with Sunday, wind gusts are forecast to be in the \n20-30 mph range and mainly from the south. Relative humidity \n(RH) values look to be in the upper teens to low 20%s. The \nlowest RH values look to be mainly west of KS Hwy 27. Moving to \nGFDI, the maximum values are forecast to be in the 30-45 range \nmainly for the northwest portion of the CWA. These values would \npromote very high fire growth. With this being said, there are \nsome concerns with whether or not all of these conditions will \noccur at the same time. \n\nMonday looks very similar to Sunday with the changes of high \ntemperatures in the low 100s for the majority of the CWA. RH \nvalues are forecast to be in the mid teens to low 20%s for the \nmajority of the CWA. The lowest values again are for areas west \nof KS Hwy 27. Gusts are once again mainly from the south and in \nthe range of 20-30 mph. GFDI values are forecast to be in the \n30-59 for the majority of the CWA. One other thing to mention is\nMonday does have a chance of seeing thunderstorms, so dry \nlightning may also pose a risk wherever storms develop.",
